id: pm-wr8utz
title: "GH-733: Windows Node 24 nightly portability regressions in author, recovery, and drift tests"
description: "Nightly Validation run 30191427127 on main 99b718ba failed three Windows Node 24 assertions: author-attribution compares native separators to a POSIX literal; recovery-context expects POSIX-rendered relocated paths; drift-scan assumes chmod makes a directory unreadable on Windows. Restore cross-platform semantic assertions without weakening production behavior."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: ci,cross-platform,gh-issue,tests,windows
created_at: "2026-07-26T07:58:00.774Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-26T17:28:53.813Z"
closed_at: "2026-07-26T08:07:53.639Z"
author: "harness:codex"
acceptance_criteria: All three failing specs use platform-correct semantic assertions; focused tests pass locally; exact pushed PR head passes the Windows Node 24 required check or nightly; GH-733 is closed with PM and exact-run evidence.
parent: pm-u9d0
severity: high
environment: GitHub Actions windows-latest / Node 24
resolution: Replaced three OS-specific test assumptions with native path construction and a deterministic inaccessible-history failure seam.
expected_result: "The Windows Node 24 nightly validates author precedence, recovery relocation, and history drift classification without OS-specific false negatives."
actual_result: "PR #734 final reviewed head e2453835 passes the required Windows Node 24 regression job, focused 27-test lane, all four coverage shards, 100% aggregate coverage, and the full hosted matrix."
regression: true
